WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.524
Webhook payloads should be treated as untrusted input until they are
validated.

2
00:00:04.524 --> 00:00:12.043
Signature verification may confirm source and integrity, but it does not
make every field meaningful, authorized, current, or safe.

3
00:00:12.043 --> 00:00:24.618
The handler should check content type, size limits, expected event type,
required fields, optional fields, unknown fields, data types, identifiers,
timestamps, and nested structure before the payload influences state.

4
00:00:24.618 --> 00:00:28.278
JSON schema concepts can help make these expectations explicit.

5
00:00:28.278 --> 00:00:30.939
Event-type allowlists are especially useful.

6
00:00:30.939 --> 00:00:39.057
A single webhook endpoint may receive many possible events, but the
application should process only the event types it intentionally supports.

7
00:00:39.057 --> 00:00:49.702
Unknown or unsupported events should be logged at an appropriate level and
acknowledged or rejected according to the integration design, without
falling through into generic processing.

8
00:00:49.702 --> 00:00:57.154
When event contracts change, schema validation and tests should make the
change visible rather than quietly accepting new meaning.

9
00:00:57.154 --> 00:01:00.614
Business logic safety requires authoritative local checks.

10
00:01:00.614 --> 00:01:11.526
Do not trust client-controlled prices, roles, permissions, account status,
approval decisions, tenant identifiers, or entitlement changes simply
because they appear in a verified payload.

11
00:01:11.526 --> 00:01:17.448
Use the event as a signal, then look up authoritative local or provider
state when the action is important.

12
00:01:17.448 --> 00:01:25.166
Confirm that the event maps to the expected tenant, organization, account,
resource, and workflow state before changing local records.

13
00:01:25.166 --> 00:01:30.489
Authorization-aware processing keeps provider identity separate from
business authority.

14
00:01:30.489 --> 00:01:36.743
A provider may be trusted to send an event, but the event still needs to
be allowed for the specific local context.

15
00:01:36.743 --> 00:01:42.997
For example, an event about one account should not affect another account
because an identifier was ambiguous.

16
00:01:42.997 --> 00:01:50.582
A subscription event should not grant access until the local system has
confirmed the relationship, status, and expected transition.

17
00:01:50.582 --> 00:01:55.240
Verified data still needs validation, authorization, and business-rule
checks.
